       The uunnsshhaarree command creates new namespaces (as specified by the
       command-line options described below) and then executes the specified
       _p_r_o_g_r_a_m. If _p_r_o_g_r_a_m is not given, then "${SHELL}" is run (default:
       _/_b_i_n_/_s_h).

       By default, a new namespace persists only as long as it has member
       processes. A new namespace can be made persistent even when it has no
       member processes by bind mounting /proc/_p_i_d/ns/_t_y_p_e files to a
       filesystem path. A namespace that has been made persistent in this way
       can subsequently be entered with nnsseenntteerr(1) even after the _p_r_o_g_r_a_m
       terminates (except PID namespaces where a permanently running init
       process is required). Once a persistent namespace is no longer needed,
       it can be unpersisted by using uummoouunntt(8) to remove the bind mount. See
       the EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS section for more details.

       uunnsshhaarree since util-linux version 2.36 uses
       _/_p_r_o_c_/_[_p_i_d_]_/_n_s_/_p_i_d___f_o_r___c_h_i_l_d_r_e_n and _/_p_r_o_c_/_[_p_i_d_]_/_n_s_/_t_i_m_e___f_o_r___c_h_i_l_d_r_e_n
       files for persistent PID and TIME namespaces. This change requires
       Linux kernel 4.17 or newer.

